{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA VERIFIED AGAINST MERGED MAIN (668523feffde), not against the agent's report. Change 2600 is MERGED with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.\n\nAC1 -- explicit timeouts and a named failure. tests/e2e/_toolchain.py:103 passes timeout=timeout to the subprocess; ToolchainProvisioningError is defined at :63; the message at :111 reads 'e2e toolchain: `{label}` timed out after {timeout:g}s in {cwd}', so the failing STEP is named rather than the worker being killed anonymously.\n\nAC2 -- portable pre-pytest provisioning. Makefile:470 defines e2e-deps. This is the no-CI-provider trigger required by project.portability; CI merely invokes it, and the in-fixture path remains as fallback.\n\nAC3 -- cache and npm ci. .github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:474 keys the cache on\n${{ runner.os }}-npm-${{ steps.e2e-node.outputs.node-version }}-${{ hashFiles('tests/e2e/js/package-lock.json') }} -- note it also includes the NODE VERSION, closing an advisory that a Node upgrade could reuse a cache built by a different toolchain. Makefile:493 runs 'npm ci'; _toolchain.py:160 documents npm install as the fallback for a tree with no lockfile.\n\nAC4 -- concurrent provisioning serialized. _toolchain.py:86 takes fcntl.flock(handle.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX), released at :94, around install+build, with a documented degradation where fcntl is absent (:27).\n\nAC5 -- verified in real time, deterministically, not by waiting for a recurrence. Cold state with node_modules and dist removed, under the CI xdist configuration: 31 passed, NO worker kill. The identical cold state on the old code killed two workers at the 300s cliff (2 failed, 12 passed, 7 skipped in 306.60s). Separately, stalling npm past the new bound yields the named AC1 failure rather than 'node down'.\n\nTHE FIX SHAPE CHANGED DURING IMPLEMENTATION, AND THE REASON IS WORTH KEEPING: a lock alone does NOT fix this. The first implementation was exactly what this ticket described -- explicit timeouts plus a lock, provisioning still inside the fixture -- and it STILL REPRODUCED, because under -n 4 --dist worksteal every worker runs the session fixture, so serialising them converts the race into a QUEUE that is still charged to a test's budget: the waiters get killed instead of the installer. Only moving provisioning out of the fixture into pytest_collection_modifyitems fixes it, because collection is not a test item and pytest-timeout does not bound it.\n\nEleven mutations killed across the change. One test was found VACUOUS by that exercise -- deleting _PROVISION_ATTEMPTED changed nothing observable, since the hook fires once per session -- and the dead mechanism was deleted rather than protected by a new test.\n\nAlso taken: playwright moved to optionalDependencies (16.8M of a 30M tree, 15 packages -> 11 with --omit=optional). Correcting my own earlier framing: playwright has NO postinstall script, so nothing downloads browsers here; the saving is package download and extract only.\n\nFollow-up filed, deliberately not folded in: 477b-4130-424d-41eb (chiselled-thrifty-gnat) -- provision_toolchain still evaluates _satisfied() BEFORE the flock, so a worker can run a bundle another is still writing. Not a regression: before this change there was no lock at all. Landing the P0 unblocker was judged worth more than resetting a green +1.\n\nNoted but not filed, pre-existing and untouched: no workflow step runs 'npx playwright install chromium', so browser_runner self-skips on 'Chromium unavailable' in CI -- an entire test tier silently skipping."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788500631552753001,"uuid":"a73b400f-8059-42c4-83e3-35962ef64615"}